  "error": null,
  "output_text": "**Introduction/Context**  \nThe study compares intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs) and normal duct tissues (NDs) using global proteome (TableS2A) and glycoproteome (TableS3I) datasets. The analysis focuses on glycopeptides with significant differential regulation (FDR < 0.05) in IPMN vs ND but with unchanged global protein expression.  \n\n**Keywords**  \nIPMN, glycoproteome, proteome, differential expression, pathway enrichment, post-translational modification, biomarker.  \n\n**Candidate Genes**  \nFrom the 37 glycoproteins with significant glycopeptide changes but stable global expression:  \n**ADAM10, CPD, MET, ERBB3, ITGA6, EPHA2, TSPAN13, ICAM2**.  \n\n**Pathway Enrichments**  \n*Not performed*: The datasets provided lack pathway annotations (e.g., KEGG, GO terms), preventing direct pathway-level analysis.  \n\n**Targeted (Keyword-Guided) Subset**  \nGenes like **ADAM10** (shedding of cell surface proteins), **MET** (oncogenic signaling), **ERBB3** (EGFR family, cancer progression), and **ITGA6** (cell adhesion) suggest roles in tumor microenvironment remodeling and signaling. These align with IPMN progression but require pathway context.  \n\n**Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)**  \n| Gene    | Direction | Role/Function                          | Pathway(s)                |  \n|---------|-----------|----------------------------------------|---------------------------|  \n| ADAM10  | Up        | Regulates Notch and EGFR signaling     | Not observed (no pathway data) |  \n| MET     | Up        | Promotes invasion and metastasis       | Not observed (no pathway data) |  \n| ITGA6   | Up        | Mediates cell-matrix adhesion          | Not observed (no pathway data) |  \n\n**Comparisons**  \nGlycoproteome changes (e.g., **CPD**, **TSPAN13**) highlight post-translational regulation absent in global proteome, suggesting IPMN-specific glycosylation alterations.  \n\n**Implications**  \nDifferential glycosylation of proteins like **ICAM2** and **ERBB3** may influence immune evasion or signaling in IPMN. These candidates warrant validation as biomarkers for progression risk.  \n\n**Next Steps & Clarification**  \n1. **Pathway analysis**: Integrate gene lists with annotated libraries (KEGG, GO) to identify enriched functions.  \n2. **Functional validation**: Test roles of **ADAM10** and **MET** in IPMN cell models.  \n3. **Tissue-specific glycosylation**: Correlate glycopeptide sites (e.g., N652 in **ANO9**) with structural/functional impacts.  \n4. **Cyst fluid validation**: Assess candidate glycoproteins in matched cyst fluid samples (not analyzed here).  \n\n**References**  \nNot applicable (no PMIDs validated in this analysis).  \n\n---  \n**Limitation**: Pathway-level conclusions could not be drawn due to missing functional annotations in the provided datasets.",
